I think the underlying idea is sound, with an important nuance.

Historical linguistics is one of the strongest tools we have for reconstructing the past because languages leave traces. Shared sound changes, borrowed words, grammatical innovations, and semantic shifts act like evidence. Linguists combine those with archaeology, genetics, and written records to build increasingly accurate historical models. None is definitive alone, but together they constrain what likely happened.

That’s very similar to your “follow the money” analogy:

Money leaves transaction trails.
Language leaves semantic trails.
Both reveal invisible structures
through accumulated traces.

The trail isn’t the truth itself—it’s evidence from which the truth can be inferred.

